                "Auditing the Allowance for Credit Losses under CECL (ASC 326) and IFRS 9: Under legacy accounting rules, bad debt reserves were calculated under the 'incurred loss' model, where reserves were established only after an actual loss event (such as a default or bankruptcy) occurred. Modern accounting standards—FASB ASC Topic 326 (Current Expected Credit Losses - CECL) and IFRS 9 (Financial Instruments)—revolutionized credit loss accounting by mandating a forward-looking Expected Loss Model. Under CECL, an enterprise must estimate expected lifetime credit losses for all trade receivables and contract assets held at the balance sheet date, incorporating historical loss experience, current macroeconomic conditions, and reasonable and supportable forward-looking forecasts (such as projected GDP growth, inflation, and industry default rates).",
